<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold, Grand Duke of Baden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold was Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> from 1830 to 1852. A comparatively liberal ruler, he allowed press freedom and reforms under Baden's constitution, and Baden joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">German Customs Union</a> in 1835. In the revolution of 1849 he was driven out by a republican uprising and returned only after Prussian troops crushed it. He was succeeded by his sons Louis II and Frederick I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-grand-duke-of-baden.jpg" alt="Leopold, Grand Duke of Baden" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Winterhalter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold,_Grand_Duke_of_Baden.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born on 29 August 1790 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, a son of Margrave Karl Friederich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> by his second, morganatic wife, Luise Karoline Geyer von Geyersberg. Because the marriage was deemed morganatic, her children were thought incapable of inheriting dynastic status, and they were made counts and countesses von Hochberg in 1796. Leopold had no prospects in Baden and followed a career as an officer in the French army.</p><p>As the male line from the first marriage died out, the position of the Hochberg children became a matter of international interest. In 1817 Grand Duke Charles confirmed their succession rights and made them princes and margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, and in 1818 he granted a constitution with a clause securing those rights. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> of 10 July 1819, signed by the Great Powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Baden, recognised them. On 25 July 1819 Leopold married Sophie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, which united the descendants of his father's two wives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Louis I died on 30 March 1830, he was the last male of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> not descended from the morganatic marriage, and Leopold succeeded as fourth Grand Duke of Baden. He took an interest in the liberal ideas of his time and granted concessions to his subjects in 1848.</p><p>In the spring of 1849 he declined to oppose the revolutionary movement, which forced him to flee the country on the night of 13 May. In August he was reinstated by the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the German Confederation. He acted with great forbearance after regaining power, and in the last years of his reign he admitted his son Frederick to a share in the government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leopold died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> on 24 April 1852. His son Louis II suffered from mental illness, so that his brother Frederick acted as regent, and Frederick I later became grand duke. Another son, Prince Wilhelm, was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Max of Baden</a>, who became German Chancellor. Since the death of the last reigning Grand Duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-grand-duke-of-baden">Frederick II</a>, in 1928, the title of Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> has been borne as a title of pretence by Leopold's senior descendant, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold, Grand Duke of Baden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,8.4&amp;year=1830" aria-label="Karlsruhe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 453 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold, Grand Duke of Baden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,8.4&amp;year=1830">What was here in 1830? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
